DESCRIPTION:The Museum of the Albemarle will host our monthly History for L
 unch on Wednesday\, Apr. 22 at 12 p.m. in the Gaither Auditorium. The Muse
 um of the Albemarle\, a part of the Division of State History Museums\, Of
 fice of Archives and History\, is an agency of the North Carolina Departme
 nt of Natural and Cultural Resources.\n\n \n\nCaptain George E. Krietemeye
 r\, US Coast Guard (Retired)\, will discuss the 235-year history of the US
  Coast Guard in North Carolina\, beginning with the arrival of the USRC Di
 ligence in 1792. Built in Washington\, NC\, to Alexander Hamilton’s desi
 gn and specifications\, its primary mission was to collect tariffs. Dilige
 nce quickly became involved in chasing pirates and aiding vessels in distr
 ess. The Coast Guard now has more than 5\,000 members working in North Car
 olina\, saving lives and overseeing oil spills all over the world. Krietem
 eyer’s new book\, Guardians of the Graveyard of the Atlantic\, describes
  their history and plans.\n\n \n\nThe Museum will offer the History for Lu
 nch program in-person and through Zoom. To attend the lecture virtually\, 
 register in advance to receive the meeting link. Registration is not requi
 red to attend the lecture in person.\n\n \n\nThe virtual program is suppor
 ted by Friends of the Museum of the Albemarle.
GEO:36.296532;-76.219757
LOCATION:Museum of the Albemarle\, Gaither Auditorium
SUMMARY:History for Lunch: Guardians of the Graveyard of the Atlantic - The
  235-Year History of the USCG in North Carolina
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/history-for-lunch-guardians-
 of-the-graveyard-of-the-atlantic-the-235-year-history-of-the-uscg-in-north
 -carolina
CATEGORIES:Education Programs
CATEGORIES:Lectures & Talks
END:VEVENT
